{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{% block title %}About the data{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
      <h1>About the data</h1>
      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Itemized receipts (Schedule A), independent expenditures (Schedule E) and committee
          history: the FEC's <a href="https://www.fec.gov/data/browse-data/?tab=bulk-data">PostgreSQL
          data dumps</a>, refreshed weekly by the FEC and here monthly.</li>
        <li>Candidates, committees and the links between them: FEC bulk files (candidate master,
          committee master, candidate-committee linkage, committee contributions to candidates).</li>
        <li>State map: US Census Bureau
          <a href="https://www.census.gov/geographies/mapping-files/time-series/geo/cartographic-boundary.html">cartographic
          boundary files</a> (public domain), as packaged by
          <a href="https://github.com/topojson/us-atlas">us-atlas</a>.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>How the numbers are built</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>"For" money is what was given to a candidate's own committees, plus independent
          expenditures reported as supporting them. "Against" is independent expenditures
          reported as opposing them.</li>
        <li>Individual donors are matched by name and ZIP code, because the FEC does not assign
          individuals an ID. Two people with the same name in the same ZIP code may be combined.</li>
        <li>Only contributions over $200 are itemized with a donor name; smaller gifts appear only
          as a total per candidate.</li>
        <li>Memo entries are left out so that earmarked gifts are not counted twice.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>How current it is</h2>
{% if data_through %}
      <p>Itemized gifts are loaded through {{ data_through | long_date }}. The FEC processes
        reports weeks after they are filed, so the newest months are incomplete, and a
        candidate who started raising money recently can show less here than on the FEC's
        own summary.</p>
{% else %}
      <p>The FEC processes reports weeks after they are filed, so the newest months are
        incomplete.</p>
{% endif %}
      <h2>Corrections made to the FEC records</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Amended reports.</strong> When a committee re-files a report in full, only
          the newest filing is counted. A smaller later filing (a partial amendment, or a
          separate report for another period) is counted alongside the original.</li>
        <li><strong>One committee, two candidate IDs.</strong> When the FEC links a committee to
          two candidate IDs in one cycle (a candidate who re-registered, or ran for another
          seat), its money is counted once, for the candidate whose committee it is in that
          election.</li>
        <li><strong>Records left out.</strong> A single "individual" gift above
          {{ max_gift | usd }} that the candidate did not give is almost always a filing error
          (one check can legitimately cover several elections, but not this much), and so are
          committees reported as individuals. These records are left out of every total and
          donor page, and are <a href="excluded.html">listed with their filings</a> so they
          can be checked.</li>
        <li><strong>Placeholder names</strong> such as "Anonymous" or "Unknown" are not people,
          so they get no donor page.</li>
        <li><strong>Totals are net of refunds.</strong> A donor whose refunds were larger than
          their gifts in the years shown has a negative total.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Charts</h2>
      <p>Every chart uses both colour and a fill pattern (stripes, dots, cross-hatching) for each
        series, and lists every value in text, so it can be read without telling colours apart.</p>
{% endblock %}
